1
00:05:31,578 --> 00:05:37,578
Which Side Are You On?

2
00:11:03,157 --> 00:11:09,157
Harlan County U.S.A. is a documentary film directed by Barbara Kopple.

3
00:16:34,736 --> 00:16:40,736
This film documents the coal miners' strike against the Brookside Mine of the
Eastover Mining Company in Harlan County, Kentucky in June, 1973.

4
00:22:06,315 --> 00:22:12,315
Eastover's refusal to sign a contract (when the miners joined with the United Mine Workers of America) led to the strike, which lasted more than a
year and included violent battles between gun-toting company thugs/scabs and the picketing miners and their supportive women-folk.

5
00:27:37,894 --> 00:27:43,894
Director Barbara Kopple puts the strike into perspective by giving us some background
on the historical plight of the miners and some history of the UMWA.

6
00:33:09,473 --> 00:33:15,473
Preserved by the Academy Film Archive in partnership with
New York Women in Film & Television in 2004.

7
00:38:41,052 --> 00:38:47,052
Self - Eastover Mining President, played by Norman Yarborough.

8
00:44:12,631 --> 00:44:18,631
Self - UMW Organizer, played by Houston Elmore.

9
00:49:44,210 --> 00:49:50,210
Self - UMW Staff, played by Phil Sparks.

10
00:55:15,789 --> 00:55:21,789
Self, played by Bessie Lou Cornett Parker.

11
01:00:47,368 --> 01:00:53,368
Self, played by Sudie Crusenberry.

12
01:06:18,947 --> 01:06:24,947
Self, played by Mary Lou Fergerson.

13
01:11:50,526 --> 01:11:56,526
Harlan County USA (variously written with and without a comma) is a 1976 American documentary film covering the "Brookside Strike", a 1973 effort of 180 coal
miners and their wives against the Duke Power Company-owned Eastover Coal Company's Brookside Mine and Prep Plant in Harlan County, southeast Kentucky.

14
01:17:22,105 --> 01:17:28,105
It won the Academy Award for Best Documentary at the 49th Academy Awards.

15
01:22:53,684 --> 01:22:59,684
It was directed and produced by filmmaker Barbara
Kopple, then early in her filmmaking career.

16
01:28:25,263 --> 01:28:31,263
A former VISTA volunteer, she had worked on other documentaries,
especially as an advocate of workers' rights.

17
01:33:56,842 --> 01:34:02,842
The film explores themes of strike, kentucky, mining, working class, union.

18
01:39:28,421 --> 01:39:34,421
The tone is dark.
